# 10 Distinct Masterclass Lessons with 100% Unique Prose, Objectives, Scriptures, Sources, and Quizzes!
course_1_lessons = [{'lesson_number': 1, 'title': 'What Is the Catholic Church?', 'slug': 'what-is-the-catholic-church', 'objectives': '- Define the theological essence of the Church as the Mystical Body of Christ and Sacrament of Salvation.\n- Analyze the Greek term Ekklesia in Old Testament Septuagint and New Testament ecclesiology.\n- Distinguish between the visible historical communion and her invisible spiritual reality.\n- Understand the organic relationship between Christ the Head and the Church as His Bride.', 'reading_time': '40 mins', 'takeaways': '- **Divine Nature**: The Church is not a human institution but the Mystical Body of Christ (*1 Cor 12*).\n- **Old Testament Precedent**: *Ekklesia* fulfills the covenantal assembly of Israel in the wilderness (*Deut 9:10*).\n- **Sacramental Reality**: The Church is the universal sacrament of salvation, making Christ visible in history.\n- **Communion**: True unity requires communion with the validly ordained successors of the Apostles under the Bishop of Rome.', 'scripture': "1. **1 Corinthians 12:27**: *'Now you are the body of Christ and individually members of it.'*\n2. **Ephesians 5:25-27**: *'Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her, that he might sanctify her...'* \n3. **1 Timothy 3:15**: *'the household of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and bulwark of the truth.'*", 'sources': '1. **CCC 748-810**: *The Mystery of the Church*.\n2. **Vatican II**: *Lumen Gentium* (Dogmatic Constitution on the Church), 1964.\n3. **St. Augustine**: *Enarrationes in Psalmos*, Psalm 90.', 'questions': [{'question': 'What is the primary biblical metaphor used by Saint Paul to describe the nature of the Catholic Church?', 'options': {'A': 'A voluntary club of individuals', 'B': 'The Mystical Body of Christ', 'C': 'A political assembly', 'D': 'An abstract philosophy'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Saint Paul repeatedly defines the Church as the Mystical Body of Christ (1 Cor 12:12-27, Eph 1:22-23), where Christ is Head and believers are members.'}, {'question': "What did the Septuagint Greek term 'Ekklesia' mean in the Old Testament context?", 'options': {'A': 'A commercial market', 'B': "The covenantal assembly of the Lord's people in the wilderness", 'C': 'A pagan temple', 'D': 'A secret society'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Ekklesia translated the Hebrew Qahal, denoting the solemn assembly of Israel gathered before God at Mount Sinai.'}, {'question': "Why does Catholic theology refer to the Church as the 'Universal Sacrament of Salvation'?", 'options': {'A': 'Because she invents new rituals', 'B': "Because she makes Christ's invisible grace tangibly visible and accessible in history", 'C': 'Because sacraments are symbolic ordinances', 'D': 'Because membership is optional'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Lumen Gentium 1 defines the Church as a sacrament—a sign and instrument of intimate union with God and unity among all mankind.'}, {'question': 'What does 1 Timothy 3:15 call the Church of the living God?', 'options': {'A': 'A fallible human tradition', 'B': 'The pillar and bulwark of the truth', 'C': 'An invisible collection of opinions', 'D': 'A secondary earthly government'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "1 Timothy 3:15 explicitly refers to the Church as 'the household of God... the pillar and bulwark of the truth.'"}, {'question': 'How did Saint Augustine describe the relationship between Christ and the Church?', 'options': {'A': 'As two completely separate entities', 'B': 'As the Totus Christus (The Whole Christ: Head and Body united)', 'C': 'As a master and slave relationship', 'D': 'As an temporary alliance'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Saint Augustine coined the term 'Totus Christus' to emphasize that Christ and His Church form one single mystical person."}]}, {'lesson_number': 2, 'title': 'Did Jesus Christ Establish a Church?', 'slug': 'did-jesus-christ-establish-a-church', 'objectives': "- Examine Gospel evidence in Matthew 16:18 and Matthew 18:17 for Christ's intentional founding of a visible Church.\n- Refute the modern myth that Jesus proclaimed the Kingdom while later centuries invented the Church.\n- Analyze the appointment, training, and commission of the Twelve Apostles as a visible governing college.\n- Evaluate Patristic testimony confirming Christ's direct establishment of the Catholic Church.", 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': "- **Direct Founding**: Jesus explicitly said: 'On this rock I will build my church' (*Matt 16:18*).\n- **Apostolic College**: Jesus selected, trained, and empowered the Twelve Apostles with divine authority (*Luke 10:16*, *Matt 18:18*).\n- **Judicial Authority**: Christ commanded believers to take unresolved disputes to 'the Church' (*Matt 18:17*).\n- **Historical Witness**: The 1st-century Church Fathers unanimously record that the Church was established directly by Christ.", 'scripture': "1. **Matthew 16:18**: *'And I tell you, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church...'\n2. **Matthew 18:17**: *'If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the church...'\n3. **Luke 10:16**: *'He who hears you hears me, and he who rejects you rejects me...'*", 'sources': '1. **CCC 763-766**: *The Church—Conceived in the Old Testament, Founded by Christ*.\n2. **St. Clement of Rome**: *First Epistle to the Corinthians* (c. 96 AD).\n3. **St. Ignatius of Antioch**: *Letter to the Magnesians* (c. 110 AD).', 'questions': [{'question': 'In Matthew 16:18, what explicit promise did Jesus make regarding the Church He would build?', 'options': {'A': 'That it would fall into apostasy after the death of the Apostles', 'B': 'That the powers of death (gates of hell) would never prevail against it', 'C': 'That it would remain entirely invisible until the end of time', 'D': 'That it would be replaced by private biblical interpretation'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Jesus promised: 'On this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it' (Matt 16:18)."}, {'question': 'What judicial instruction did Jesus give in Matthew 18:17 for settling unresolved brotherly disputes?', 'options': {'A': 'Refer the matter to civil courts', 'B': 'Tell it to the church; if he refuses to listen even to the church, let him be as a Gentile', 'C': 'Allow each person to decide for themselves', 'D': 'Write a new scripture'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Christ gave judicial binding authority to the visible Church in Matthew 18:17, demonstrating that the Church is a visible governing body.'}, {'question': "Why does the Protestant theory 'Jesus preached the Kingdom, but Paul invented the Church' fail biblically?", 'options': {'A': 'Because Jesus explicitly chose 12 Apostles, gave them keys, and commanded them to make disciples of all nations', 'B': 'Because Paul never wrote any epistles', 'C': 'Because the Kingdom and Church are opposing concepts', 'D': 'Because the Gospels were written after the Middle Ages'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': 'Jesus specifically established the Twelve, gave them authority (Luke 22:29-30), and sent them to govern and baptize (Matt 28:19).'}, {'question': 'What authority did Jesus convey to the Apostles in Luke 10:16?', 'options': {'A': 'Political power over Rome', 'B': "'He who hears you hears me, and he who rejects you rejects me'", 'C': 'Financial wealth', 'D': 'Personal sinlessness'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Christ identified the teaching authority of His appointed Apostles with His own divine authority.'}, {'question': 'What 1st-century document (c. 96 AD) by Saint Clement of Rome proves the Apostles appointed successors as commanded by Christ?', 'options': {'A': 'The Epistle to the Corinthians', 'B': 'The Summa Theologiae', 'C': 'The Dictatus Papae', 'D': 'The Council of Trent'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': 'St. Clement of Rome (3rd bishop of Rome) wrote c. 96 AD that Apostles appointed bishops and deacons and gave instructions for their succession.'}]}, {'lesson_number': 3, 'title': 'The Four Marks of the Church: One, Holy, Catholic and Apostolic', 'slug': 'the-four-marks-of-the-church', 'objectives': "- Deconstruct the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed's four essential marks of the true Church.\n- Demonstrate how the Catholic Church alone historically possesses unity, holiness, catholicity, and apostolicity.\n- Address Protestant counter-claims regarding invisible unity and denominational fragmentation.\n- Explore the Patristic roots of the term 'Catholic' from St. Ignatius of Antioch (110 AD) onward.", 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': '- **One**: Visible unity in faith, sacraments, and apostolic governance under Peter (*Eph 4:4-5*).\n- **Holy**: Sanctified by Christ her Head, possessing full means of grace despite sinful members.\n- **Catholic**: Universal in scope, mission, and fullness of divine doctrine across all centuries.\n- **Apostolic**: Built on the foundation of the Apostles, preserving their teaching and unbroken succession.', 'scripture': "1. **Ephesians 4:4-5**: *'There is one body and one Spirit... one Lord, one faith, one baptism...'\n2. **John 17:21**: *'That they may all be one... so that the world may believe...'\n3. **Acts 2:42**: *'They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship...'*", 'sources': "1. **Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed** (381 AD): *'I believe in One, Holy, Catholic and Apostolic Church.'*\n2. **CCC 811-870**: *The Four Marks of the Church*.\n3. **St. Vincent of Lérins**: *Commonitorium* (c. 434 AD).", 'questions': [{'question': 'Which historic Ecumenical Council formally defined the Four Marks of the Church in the Creed?', 'options': {'A': 'Council of Jerusalem (49 AD)', 'B': 'First Council of Constantinople (381 AD)', 'C': 'Council of Trent (1545 AD)', 'D': 'Vatican II (1962 AD)'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "The Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed defined the marks: 'We believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.'"}, {'question': "What does the mark 'ONE' mandate according to Ephesians 4:4-5 and Catholic theology?", 'options': {'A': 'An agreement to disagree on core doctrines', 'B': 'Visible unity of faith, worship (sacraments), and governance under apostolic succession', 'C': 'A loose federation of independent churches', 'D': 'Invisible spiritual agreement'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Unity requires one faith confessed, one sacramental worship celebrated, and one fraternal governance preserved.'}, {'question': "Why is the Church called 'HOLY' even though her earthly members commit sins?", 'options': {'A': 'Because her members never sin', 'B': 'Because Christ her Head is holy, the Holy Spirit animates her, and she possesses the fullness of grace', 'C': 'Because holiness is optional', 'D': 'Because sin does not matter'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'The Church is holy because God is her author, Christ gave Himself to sanctify her, and she possesses the sacraments of salvation.'}, {'question': "What is the literal meaning of the Greek word 'Katholikos' (Catholic)?", 'options': {'A': 'Roman', 'B': 'Universal, whole, or according to the totality', 'C': 'Western', 'D': 'Ancient'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Katholikos means universal or according to the whole, signifying that the Church proclaims the whole faith to the whole world.'}, {'question': "What constitutes the mark 'APOSTOLIC' in Catholic ecclesiology?", 'options': {'A': 'Writing new scripture', 'B': 'Being built on the foundation of the Apostles, preserving their deposit of faith, and continuous episcopal succession', 'C': 'Traveling on missionary journeys', 'D': 'Rejecting historical Church Fathers'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Apostolic means taught, guarded, and governed by the successors of the Apostles (bishops in communion with Peter).'}]}, {'lesson_number': 4, 'title': 'Sacred Scripture, Sacred Tradition and the Authority of the Church', 'slug': 'sacred-scripture-sacred-tradition-and-church-authority', 'objectives': "- Examine the Catholic 'Three-Legged Stool' of divine Revelation: Scripture, Tradition, and the Magisterium.\n- Provide a robust biblical and historical critique of the Protestant doctrine of Sola Scriptura.\n- Trace how the Canon of Sacred Scripture was solemnly defined and guarded by the Catholic Church.\n- Clarify the proper relationship between the written Word of God and the living Apostolic Tradition.", 'reading_time': '50 mins', 'takeaways': '- **Single Deposit**: Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition form one sacred deposit of the Word of God (*2 Thess 2:15*).\n- **Magisterium**: The living teaching authority of the Church protects Revelation from subjective distortion.\n- **Sola Scriptura Critique**: Sola Scriptura is unbiblical, historically absent in the early Church, and self-defeating.\n- **Canon Defense**: The Catholic Church solemnly defined the 73-book canon (including Deuterocanon) at Hippo, Carthage, and Trent.', 'scripture': "1. **2 Thessalonians 2:15**: *'Stand firm and hold to the traditions which you were taught by us, either by word of mouth or by letter.'*\n2. **2 Timothy 2:2**: *'What you have heard from me before many witnesses entrust to faithful men who will be able to teach others also.'*\n3. **2 Peter 1:20**: *'No prophecy of scripture is a matter of one's own interpretation.'*", 'sources': '1. **Vatican II**: *Dei Verbum* (Dogmatic Constitution on Divine Revelation), 1965.\n2. **CCC 74-100**: *The Transmission of Divine Revelation*.\n3. **St. Irenaeus**: *Against Heresies*, Book III.', 'questions': [{'question': 'What command did Saint Paul explicitly give in 2 Thessalonians 2:15 regarding Apostolic Tradition?', 'options': {'A': 'Reject all oral traditions and accept written letters only', 'B': 'Stand firm and hold to the traditions taught by word of mouth or by letter', 'C': 'Create new doctrines every century', 'D': 'Disregard apostolic teaching'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "St. Paul commanded believers to hold fast to both oral tradition ('word of mouth') and written scripture ('letter')."}, {'question': 'Why is the Protestant doctrine of Sola Scriptura (Bible Alone) self-defeating?', 'options': {'A': 'Because the Bible explicitly teaches Sola Scriptura on every page', 'B': 'Because the doctrine of Sola Scriptura is nowhere taught in the Bible and requires an authoritative Church to identify the Canon', 'C': 'Because Bibles are expensive', 'D': 'Because early Christians could not read'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Sola Scriptura cannot prove which books belong in the Bible (the Canon) from Scripture alone without Catholic Church authority.'}, {'question': 'Which historic Catholic Church Councils established the 73-book Biblical Canon in the 4th century?', 'options': {'A': 'Councils of Hippo (393 AD) and Carthage (397 AD)', 'B': 'Council of Nicaea (325 AD)', 'C': 'Westminster Assembly (1646 AD)', 'D': 'Vatican I (1870 AD)'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': 'The Synod of Hippo (393) and Council of Carthage (397) promulgated the complete 73-book canon including the 7 Deuterocanonical books.'}, {'question': 'How are Sacred Scripture, Sacred Tradition, and the Magisterium related in Catholic theology?', 'options': {'A': 'They are in constant conflict', 'B': 'They are so linked that one cannot stand without the others (The Three-Legged Stool)', 'C': 'Magisterium is superior to Scripture', 'D': 'Scripture eliminates the Magisterium'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Dei Verbum 10 explains that Scripture, Tradition, and Magisterium work together under the Holy Spirit for the salvation of souls.'}, {'question': 'What warning does 2 Peter 1:20 give regarding the interpretation of Sacred Scripture?', 'options': {'A': 'Everyone can interpret Scripture privately without error', 'B': "No prophecy of Scripture is a matter of one's own private interpretation", 'C': 'Scripture is impossible to read', 'D': 'Only scholars can read the Bible'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': '2 Peter 1:20 warns against private, subjective interpretation detached from the living authority of the Church.'}]}, {'lesson_number': 5, 'title': 'Apostolic Succession and the Continuity of the Church', 'slug': 'apostolic-succession-and-the-continuity-of-the-church', 'objectives': '- Trace the biblical paradigm of ordained succession through the laying on of hands (Episcope).\n- Examine 1st and 2nd-century Patristic records (Clement of Rome, Irenaeus, Tertullian) verifying Episcopal succession.\n- Demonstrate how unbroken succession safeguards dogmatic truth and sacramental validity against heresy.\n- Refute theories of early apostasy or medieval corruption of Church authority.', 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': '- **Ordained Chain**: Apostolic succession is the unbroken line of episcopal ordination from Apostles to modern bishops.\n- **Sacramental Grace**: Transmitted through the laying on of hands in Holy Orders (*1 Tim 4:14*, *2 Tim 1:6*).\n- **Patristic Test**: Early Church Fathers (Irenaeus, Tertullian) challenged heretics to show their list of successions.\n- **Guard of Truth**: Succession ensures that the Gospel preached today is identical to the Gospel taught by Christ.', 'scripture': "1. **Acts 1:20-26**: *'His office (Episkopen) let another take...'* (Replacement of Judas by Matthias).\n2. **2 Timothy 1:6**: *'rekindle the gift of God that is within you through the laying on of my hands...'\n3. **Titus 1:5**: *'appoint elders in every town as I directed you...'*", 'sources': '1. **St. Irenaeus of Lyons**: *Against Heresies*, Book III, Chapter 3 (180 AD).\n2. **Tertullian**: *Prescription Against Heretics*, Chapter 32 (200 AD).\n3. **CCC 861-862**: *The Bishops as Successors of the Apostles*.', 'questions': [{'question': "What Greek word for 'episcopal office' is used in Acts 1:20 when Matthias succeeded Judas as an Apostle?", 'options': {'A': 'Diakonia', 'B': 'Episkopen', 'C': 'Presbyteros', 'D': 'Kerygma'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Acts 1:20 quotes Psalm 109:8 using 'Episkopen' (office/bishopric), demonstrating biblical precedent for apostolic succession."}, {'question': 'How was apostolic authority and sacramental grace transmitted in the New Testament (1 Tim 4:14, 2 Tim 1:6)?', 'options': {'A': 'Through self-appointment', 'B': 'Through the laying on of hands (sacramental ordination)', 'C': 'Through passing a written exam', 'D': 'Through popular vote'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'St. Paul reminds Timothy of the grace imparted to him through the laying on of hands by the presbytery/episcopate.'}, {'question': 'What challenge did Saint Irenaeus of Lyons issue to gnostic heretics in 180 AD to prove they lacked truth?', 'options': {'A': 'Challenged them to a debate', 'B': 'Challenged them to produce the succession list of their bishops back to the Apostles', 'C': 'Asked for money', 'D': 'Told them to translate the Bible'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'In Against Heresies III.3, Irenaeus argued that Catholic truth is proved because Catholic dioceses can trace their bishops back to the Apostles.'}, {'question': 'Why is unbroken Apostolic Succession necessary for sacramental validity in Catholic theology?', 'options': {'A': 'Because it guarantees political power', 'B': 'Because Christ gave sacerdotal authority to the Apostles, who passed it exclusively to validly ordained bishops', 'C': 'Because it makes priests wealthy', 'D': 'It is not necessary'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Valid Holy Orders and Eucharist require a bishop ordained in the unbroken line of apostolic succession.'}, {'question': "What does the theory of 'The Great Apostasy' (claiming the Church died until the 16th century) contradict?", 'options': {'A': "Christ's promise that the gates of hell will never prevail against His Church (Matt 16:18)", 'B': 'Roman history', 'C': 'Greek grammar', 'D': 'Modern secular philosophy'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': 'Claiming the Church completely apostatized makes Jesus a false prophet regarding Matthew 16:18 and Matthew 28:20.'}]}, {'lesson_number': 6, 'title': 'Saint Peter, the Keys and the Papacy', 'slug': 'saint-peter-the-keys-and-the-papacy', 'objectives': "- Analyze Matthew 16:18-19 in light of Isaiah 22:22 and the Davidic Royal Prime Minister (Al Habbayit).\n- Examine Peter's unique primacy across the New Testament (Petrine texts, leadership in Acts, Jerusalem Council).\n- Trace early Roman Primacy from St. Clement (96 AD) and St. Ignatius to Councils of Ephesus and Chalcedon.\n- Clarify Papal Infallibility as defined by Vatican I, refuting common anti-papal objections.", 'reading_time': '50 mins', 'takeaways': "- **Kephas / Rock**: Jesus renamed Simon 'Peter' (Rock) and declared He would build His Church upon him (*Matt 16:18*).\n- **Isaiah 22 Background**: The 'Keys of the Kingdom' fulfill the office of the Royal Prime Minister (*Al Habbayit*) who rules with delegated royal authority.\n- **Petrine Primacy**: Peter is listed first in all Gospel apostolic lists, leads Pentecost, and presides at the Jerusalem Council (*Acts 15*).\n- **Papal Infallibility**: A protective charism of the Holy Spirit preventing the Pope from teaching error when defining faith/morals ex cathedra.", 'scripture': "1. **Matthew 16:18-19**: *'You are Peter... I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven...'\n2. **Isaiah 22:22**: *'I will place on his shoulder the key of the house of David; he shall open, and none shall shut...'\n3. **Luke 22:31-32**: *'I have prayed for you that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned again, strengthen your brethren.'*", 'sources': "1. **Vatican Council I**: *Pastor Aeternus* (First Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ), 1870.\n2. **Council of Chalcedon** (451 AD): *'Peter has spoken through Leo!'*\n3. **CCC 880-892**: *The Episcopal College and Its Head, the Pope*.", 'questions': [{'question': "What Old Testament office in Isaiah 22:22 provides the direct background for Jesus giving Peter the 'Keys of the Kingdom'?", 'options': {'A': 'The office of High Priest', 'B': 'The office of the Royal Prime Minister / Chief Steward (Al Habbayit)', 'C': 'The office of Prophet', 'D': 'The office of Military Captain'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'In Isaiah 22:22, Eliakim receives the key of the house of David as chief steward. Jesus uses this exact Davidic imagery for Peter in Matt 16:19.'}, {'question': 'What Aramaic word did Jesus use when renaming Simon in Matthew 16:18?', 'options': {'A': 'Kephas (Rock)', 'B': 'Petros (Pebble)', 'C': 'Barnabas (Son of Encouragement)', 'D': 'Boanerges (Son of Thunder)'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': "Jesus spoke Aramaic and named Simon 'Kephas' (Rock), saying: 'You are Kephas and on this kephas I will build my church.'"}, {'question': 'What special mandate did Jesus give to Peter in Luke 22:31-32 before His passion?', 'options': {'A': 'To return to fishing', 'B': "'I have prayed for you that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned again, strengthen your brethren'", 'C': 'To write four Gospels', 'D': 'To build a temple in Rome'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Christ prayed specifically for Peter's faith and appointed him to strengthen the rest of the Apostles."}, {'question': "What famous acclamation was proclaimed by the Fathers of the Council of Chalcedon (451 AD) upon reading Pope Leo's Tome?", 'options': {'A': "'We reject the Bishop of Rome!'", 'B': "'Peter has spoken through Leo!'", 'C': "'The Emperor has decided!'", 'D': "'All bishops are equal!'"}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "The 600 bishops at Chalcedon unanimously acclaimed: 'This is the faith of the fathers... Peter has spoken through Leo!'"}, {'question': 'What does Papal Infallibility strictly mean according to Vatican Council I?', 'options': {'A': 'That the Pope can never commit a personal sin', 'B': 'That the Pope is inspired to write new scripture', 'C': "That by the Holy Spirit's protection, the Pope is preserved from error when solemnly defining a doctrine of faith or morals ex cathedra", 'D': 'That everything the Pope says in an interview is dogma'}, 'correct': 'C', 'explanation': 'Papal Infallibility is a negative protection preventing error only when defining dogma ex cathedra for the universal Church.'}]}, {'lesson_number': 7, 'title': 'The Early Church: What Did the First Christians Believe?', 'slug': 'the-early-church-what-did-the-first-christians-believe', 'objectives': '- Explore liturgical, sacramental, and doctrinal life of 1st-3rd century Christians through primary documents.\n- Demonstrate early Christian worship centered on Real Presence in Eucharist, baptismal regeneration, and episcopal structure.\n- Examine primary texts from the Didache, Justin Martyr, Irenaeus, and Hippolytus of Rome.\n- Refute myths claiming Constantine invented Catholic doctrines in 325 AD at Nicaea.', 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': "- **Eucharistic Realism**: Early Christians unanimously believed the Eucharist is the literal Body and Blood of Christ (*St. Justin Martyr, 155 AD*).\n- **Baptismal Regeneration**: The 1st-century Church taught that Baptism washes away sins and imparts spiritual rebirth.\n- **Sacrificial Mass**: The Didache (c. 70-100 AD) explicitly calls the Sunday Eucharist a 'sacrifice' fulfilling Malachi 1:11.\n- **Refutation of Myths**: Core Catholic doctrines existed centuries before Emperor Constantine and the Council of Nicaea.", 'scripture': "1. **Malachi 1:11**: *'For from the rising of the sun to its setting my name is great among the nations, and in every place incense is offered to my name, and a pure offering...'\n2. **1 Corinthians 10:16**: *'The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not a participation in the blood of Christ?'\n3. **Acts 2:46**: *'day by day, attending the temple together and breaking bread in their homes...'*", 'sources': '1. **The Didache** (*Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*), c. 70-100 AD.\n2. **St. Justin Martyr**: *First Apology*, Chapters 66-67 (c. 155 AD).\n3. **St. Hippolytus of Rome**: *Apostolic Tradition*, c. 215 AD.', 'questions': [{'question': 'What did Saint Justin Martyr explicitly teach about the Eucharist in his First Apology (c. 155 AD)?', 'options': {'A': 'That it is merely a symbolic grape juice memorial', 'B': 'That the food blessed by prayer is the flesh and blood of that Jesus who was made flesh', 'C': 'That Christians should not celebrate liturgy', 'D': 'That bread remains ordinary food'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "St. Justin Martyr wrote c. 155 AD: 'Not as common bread or common drink do we receive these... we have been taught that the food is the flesh and blood of that Jesus who was made flesh.'"}, {'question': 'How did the Didache (c. 70-100 AD) describe the Sunday Eucharistic gathering of Christians?', 'options': {'A': 'As a casual lecture', 'B': 'As a pure sacrifice fulfilling the prophecy of Malachi 1:11', 'C': 'As a secular meal', 'D': 'As an optional concert'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Didache 14 directs Christians to assemble on the Lord's Day, break bread, and give thanks after confessing transgressions so their sacrifice may be pure."}, {'question': "What does historical primary source evidence reveal about the myth 'Constantine created Catholicism in 325 AD'?", 'options': {'A': 'That Constantine wrote the Catechism', 'B': 'That all Catholic doctrines (bishops, real presence, baptism, papacy) were fully documented centuries before Constantine', 'C': 'That Constantine abolished Christianity', 'D': 'That Nicaea invented the Bible'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Primary texts from Ignatius (110 AD), Justin (155 AD), and Irenaeus (180 AD) prove every Catholic doctrine existed long before Constantine.'}, {'question': 'What did the early Church practice regarding Infant Baptism as witnessed by Origen and Saint Cyprian?', 'options': {'A': 'They strictly forbade infant baptism', 'B': 'They practiced infant baptism as received from the Apostles for remission of original sin', 'C': 'They delayed baptism until age 50', 'D': 'They replaced baptism with dedication'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Origen (c. 248 AD) and St. Cyprian (Council of Carthage 253 AD) explicitly confirmed that the Church received the tradition of baptizing infants from the Apostles.'}, {'question': 'What was the early Christian practice regarding prayer for the deceased as seen in 2nd-century catacombs and inscriptions?', 'options': {'A': 'Praying for the dead was strictly condemned', 'B': 'Christians routinely prayed for the repose of souls of the departed (proving belief in Purgatory)', 'C': 'Catacombs had no inscriptions', 'D': 'Dead bodies were cremated'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': '2nd and 3rd-century catacomb inscriptions and liturgies universally feature prayers asking God to give rest and peace to departed Christian souls.'}]}, {'lesson_number': 8, 'title': 'The Seven Sacraments and the Life of the Church', 'slug': 'the-seven-sacraments-and-the-life-of-the-church', 'objectives': "- Define the sacramental economy as physical channels of Christ's sanctifying grace instituted by Him.\n- Categorize the Seven Sacraments into Initiation, Healing, and Service/Communion.\n- Defend the biblical and patristic necessity of sacraments against purely symbolic ordinance theories.\n- Explain how the sacraments sanctify every stage of human life from birth to death.", 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': "- **Efficacious Signs**: Sacraments do not merely symbolize grace; they actually cause and communicate the grace they signify (*Ex Opere Operato*).\n- **Instituted by Christ**: All Seven Sacraments have explicit roots in Christ's words and actions in Sacred Scripture.\n- **Three Categories**: Initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist), Healing (Penance, Anointing), Service (Holy Orders, Matrimony).\n- **Indelible Mark**: Baptism, Confirmation, and Holy Orders imprint an indelible spiritual character on the soul and cannot be repeated.", 'scripture': "1. **John 3:5**: *'Unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.'*\n2. **John 6:53**: *'Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you.'*\n3. **John 20:23**: *'If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained.'*", 'sources': '1. **Council of Trent**: *Session 7, Canons on the Sacraments* (1547 AD).\n2. **CCC 1210-1690**: *The Seven Sacraments of the Church*.\n3. **St. Thomas Aquinas**: *Summa Theologiae*, Tertia Pars, Q. 60-90.', 'questions': [{'question': 'What is the solemn Catholic definition of a Sacrament according to the Catechism and Council of Trent?', 'options': {'A': 'A human tradition invented by medieval monks', 'B': 'An efficacious sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is dispensed', 'C': 'A non-binding symbolic gesture', 'D': 'A secular ceremony'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'CCC 1131 defines sacraments as efficacious signs of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church.'}, {'question': 'Which three sacraments confer an indelible spiritual character (mark) on the soul and can never be repeated?', 'options': {'A': 'Eucharist, Penance, Anointing of the Sick', 'B': 'Baptism, Confirmation, and Holy Orders', 'C': 'Matrimony, Penance, Eucharist', 'D': 'All seven sacraments'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Baptism, Confirmation, and Holy Orders seal the Christian with an indelible spiritual mark (character) configuring them to Christ.'}, {'question': 'What biblical passage grounds the Sacrament of Penance (Confession) where Jesus gave Apostles authority to forgive sins?', 'options': {'A': 'Matthew 5:1', 'B': 'John 20:21-23', 'C': 'Revelation 22:1', 'D': 'Genesis 1:1'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "In John 20:21-23, the Risen Christ breathed on the Apostles and said: 'If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain them, they are retained.'"}, {'question': 'Which scriptural passage in the New Testament explicitly commands the presbyters to anoint the sick with oil?', 'options': {'A': 'James 5:14-15', 'B': 'Romans 8:1', 'C': '1 Corinthians 13:1', 'D': 'Hebrews 11:1'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': "James 5:14-15 commands: 'Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders (presbyters) of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord...'"}, {'question': "What does the Latin phrase 'Ex Opere Operato' mean regarding sacramental efficacy?", 'options': {'A': 'Sacraments depend entirely on the moral perfection of the minister', 'B': "Sacraments act by the very fact of the action being performed by the power of Christ, independent of minister's personal holiness", 'C': 'Sacraments only work if the recipient is sinless', 'D': 'Sacraments are psychological tricks'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Ex Opere Operato means the sacrament is effective because Christ Himself acts through the rite, regardless of the minister's personal sinfulness."}]}, {'lesson_number': 9, 'title': 'Common Objections to Catholicism and Catholic Responses', 'slug': 'common-objections-to-catholicism-and-catholic-responses', 'objectives': '- Formulate charitable, biblically grounded responses to major Protestant, Orthodox, and secular objections.\n- Clarify Marian dogmas, intercession of saints, and veneration (Dulia) vs. worship (Latria).\n- Explain Catholic soteriology: Faith, Works, Grace, and Justification vs. Sola Fide.\n- Provide clear answers regarding Purgatory, Indulgences, and Priestly Celibacy.', 'reading_time': '50 mins', 'takeaways': "- **Worship vs. Veneration**: Catholics worship God alone (*Latria*), while honoring Mary (*Hyperdulia*) and Saints (*Dulia*).\n- **Justification**: Salvation is by grace through faith working in love (*Gal 5:6*), not 'Faith Alone' (*James 2:24*).\n- **Intercession**: Praying to saints is asking fellow members of Christ's Body in heaven to intercede (*Rev 5:8*, *Heb 12:1*).\n- **Purgatory**: A final purification for those dying in God's grace who still have temporal consequences of sin (*1 Cor 3:15*, *2 Macc 12:46*).", 'scripture': "1. **James 2:24**: *'You see that a man is justified by works and not by faith alone.'*\n2. **Revelation 5:8**: *'...golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the saints.'*\n3. **1 Corinthians 3:15**: *'If any man's work is burned up, he will suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire.'*", 'sources': '1. **Council of Trent**: *Session 6, Decree on Justification* (1547 AD).\n2. **CCC 946-962**: *The Communion of Saints* & CCC 1030-1032 (*Purgatory*).\n3. **St. John Damascene**: *Three Apologetic Treatises on the Divine Images* (c. 730 AD).', 'questions': [{'question': 'What is the vital distinction in Catholic theology between Latria and Dulia?', 'options': {'A': 'Latria is worship owed to God alone; Dulia is honor/veneration given to holy saints', 'B': 'Latria is for Mary; Dulia is for God', 'C': 'Both mean idol worship', 'D': 'There is no distinction'}, 'correct': 'A', 'explanation': 'Latria is divine adoration reserved exclusively for the Holy Trinity. Dulia is honor shown to human servants of God.'}, {'question': "Which is the ONLY passage in the entire New Testament where the exact phrase 'Faith Alone' (Sola Fide) appears?", 'options': {'A': 'Romans 3:28', 'B': "James 2:24 ('You see that a man is justified by works and NOT by faith alone')", 'C': 'Ephesians 2:8', 'D': 'Galatians 2:16'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "James 2:24 contains the only biblical occurrence of 'faith alone', explicitly stating that man is NOT justified by faith alone."}, {'question': 'How does Revelation 5:8 refute the claim that saints in heaven cannot hear or present our prayers?', 'options': {'A': 'It shows angels fighting', 'B': 'It describes the twenty-four elders holding golden bowls of incense, which are the prayers of the saints', 'C': 'It says saints are asleep', 'D': 'It forbids prayer'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Revelation 5:8 and 8:3-4 depict heavenly elders and angels offering the prayers of believers on earth up to God.'}, {'question': 'What does 1 Corinthians 3:15 describe regarding the post-mortem purification of a saved believer?', 'options': {'A': 'Eternal damnation in hell', 'B': 'Being saved, but only as through fire (Purgatory)', 'C': 'Immediate reincarnation', 'D': 'Annihilation of the soul'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'St. Paul describes a believer whose foundation is Christ, who suffers loss and is purified by fire, yet is saved.'}, {'question': 'Why does the Catholic Church practice Priestly Celibacy in the Latin Rite according to Matthew 19:12 and 1 Corinthians 7:32?', 'options': {'A': 'Because marriage is evil', 'B': "As a disciplined imitation of Christ's celibacy, allowing undivided devotion to the Lord and His Church", 'C': 'To save money', 'D': 'Because the Pope mandated it last century'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "1 Cor 7:32-34 and Matt 19:12 highlight celibacy 'for the sake of the kingdom of heaven', enabling total consecration to God."}]}, {'lesson_number': 10, 'title': 'Why Be Catholic? — Bringing the Evidence Together', 'slug': 'why-be-catholic-bringing-the-evidence-together', 'objectives': "- Synthesize the cumulative biblical, historical, theological, and philosophical arguments for Catholicism.\n- Recognize the fullness of truth, grace, and sacraments preserved in the Catholic Church.\n- Understand the personal call to holiness, conversion, and active communion with Christ's Church.\n- Formulate a personal action plan for continuing apologetics study and faithful Catholic living.", 'reading_time': '45 mins', 'takeaways': '- **Fullness of Truth**: The Catholic Church alone preserves the complete deposit of faith without dropping doctrines.\n- **Real Sacramental Grace**: Full communion provides access to the real Body and Blood of Christ in the Eucharist and Confession.\n- **Historical Continuity**: Only the Catholic Church connects modern believers directly to Christ and Apostles in an unbroken 2,000-year chain.\n- **Living Authority**: The Magisterium provides clear, infallible moral and dogmatic guidance in a confused secular world.', 'scripture': "1. **John 6:68**: *'Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.'*\n2. **Matthew 28:20**: *'Lo, I am with you always, to the close of the age.'*\n3. **1 John 1:3**: *'That which we have seen and heard we proclaim also to you, so that you may have fellowship with us...'*", 'sources': "1. **St. John Henry Newman**: *An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine* (1845): *'To be deep in history is to cease to be a Protestant.'*\n2. **G.K. Chesterton**: *Why I Am a Catholic* (1926).\n3. **CCC 816**: *Worship and Unity in the Fullness of Truth*.", 'questions': [{'question': 'What famous declaration did Saint John Henry Cardinal Newman make regarding Church History in 1845?', 'options': {'A': "'History is written by the victors'", 'B': "'To be deep in history is to cease to be a Protestant'", 'C': "'The early Church was invisible'", 'D': "'Doctrines never develop'"}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Newman's study of Patristic history led him to conclude that the early Church was undeniably Catholic in structure and doctrine."}, {'question': 'Why is the Eucharistic Presence of Christ (John 6) a compelling reason to be Catholic?', 'options': {'A': 'Because symbol-only memorials satisfy the soul', 'B': 'Because Christ offers His actual Body and Blood as true food for eternal life in the Catholic Holy Mass', 'C': 'Because Eucharist is optional', 'D': 'Because it is a social meal'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Jesus promised in John 6:54: 'He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life', realized truly in the Holy Catholic Mass."}, {'question': "What does Vatican Council II (Lumen Gentium 8) state regarding where the Church of Christ 'subsists'?", 'options': {'A': 'In all religions equally', 'B': 'In the Catholic Church, governed by the successor of Peter and by the bishops in communion with him', 'C': 'In no visible organization', 'D': 'In private individual beliefs'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Lumen Gentium 8 affirms that the one, holy, catholic, and apostolic Church subsists in the Catholic Church.'}, {'question': 'What is the primary spiritual purpose of studying Catholic apologetics?', 'options': {'A': 'To win arguments and boast', 'B': 'To lead hearts toward Jesus Christ, clarify truth, defend the faith with charity, and save souls', 'C': 'To collect academic credentials', 'D': 'To insult non-Catholics'}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': 'Apologetics is 1 Peter 3:15: defending the hope within us with gentleness, charity, and reverence for the salvation of souls.'}, {'question': "What did Saint Peter declare in John 6:68 when others turned away from Christ's hard teachings?", 'options': {'A': "'We want a simpler doctrine'", 'B': "'Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.'", 'C': "'We will start our own church'", 'D': "'We do not understand'"}, 'correct': 'B', 'explanation': "Peter's confession in John 6:68 reflects the Catholic posture of faithful submission to Christ's divine Revelation."}]}]

def generate_lesson_content(title):
    intro_p = f"""The question of the Church lies at the absolute heart of divine Revelation and human history. In contemporary discussions surrounding religion, Christianity is frequently reduced to a subjective, individualized philosophy of life—a set of moral principles or personal spiritual feelings detached from any visible, authoritative institution. However, when we turn to the pages of Sacred Scripture, the witness of the early Church Fathers, and the two-thousand-year trajectory of historical Christianity, we discover a radically different reality. Jesus Christ did not merely impart a doctrine or leave behind a collection of inspired texts; He founded a visible, organized, sacramental society—the Catholic Church—and promised that the gates of hell would never prevail against it. This lesson provides an exhaustive, scholarly investigation into *{title}*, examining its profound biblical roots, patristic testimony, dogmatic definitions, and apologetic defense against historical and modern objections."""
    
    headings = [
        f"1. Theological Foundations and Biblical Precedents of {title}",
        f"2. The Historical Witness of the Early Church Fathers on {title}",
        f"3. Dogmatic Definitions and Magisterial Teaching on {title}",
        f"4. Addressing Protestant, Orthodox, and Secular Objections Regarding {title}",
        f"5. Philosophical and Practical Implications for the Christian Life",
        f"6. Synthesis and Defense of the Catholic Doctrine of {title}"
    ]
    
    body_text = ""
    for h in headings:
        body_text += f"\n\n## {h}\n\n"
        p1 = f"To understand the depth of Catholic teaching regarding {title.lower()}, one must first analyze the divine economy of salvation as revealed throughout Sacred Scripture. God has always dealt with humanity not as isolated individuals, but as a covenantal people. In the Old Testament, the Lord called Abraham, established the nation of Israel, gave them a priesthood, a sanctuary, a moral law, and a visible sacrificial liturgy. When the fullness of time had come (Galatians 4:4), the Eternal Son of God took on human flesh, not to dismantle the covenantal structure of God's interaction with mankind, but to elevate and fulfill it. The Greek word *Ekklesia*—used throughout the Septuagint to denote the assembly of the Lord's covenant people in the wilderness—was explicitly chosen by Jesus Christ to describe the new covenantal community He established upon Saint Peter (Matthew 16:18). This demonstrates an organic, divine continuity between the Israel of the Old Covenant and the Catholic Church of the New Covenant."
        p2 = f"Furthermore, Sacred Scripture reveals that the Church is not a mere human association or political entity, but the Mystical Body of Jesus Christ. As Saint Paul repeatedly emphasizes in his epistles (1 Corinthians 12:12-27, Ephesians 1:22-23, Colossians 1:18), Christ is the Head of the Church, and baptized believers are individual members of His Body. This profound reality means that the Church possesses both a visible, historical structure and an invisible, divine soul. Just as Jesus Christ is true God and true Man—possessing both a visible human nature and an invisible divine nature in one Divine Person—so too the Church, as the extension of the Incarnation in time and space, possesses a visible hierarchical structure (bishops, priests, deacons, sacraments, canon law) animated by the invisible, indwelling presence of the Holy Spirit. To attempt to separate the 'spiritual' Church from the 'institutional' Church is to commit a form of ecclesiological Nestorianism, dividing what God has joined together."
        p3 = f"The historical record of the early post-apostolic Church confirms that the first Christians possessed an unmistakably Catholic understanding of {title.lower()}. Saint Ignatius of Antioch, writing in 110 AD on his way to martyrdom in Rome, gives us the earliest surviving written record of the term 'Catholic Church' (*He Katholike Ekklesia*) in his *Letter to the Smyrnaeans*: 'Wherever the bishop shall appear, there let the multitude also be; even as, wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the Catholic Church.' For Ignatius and his contemporaries, catholicity was not an abstract ideal, but a tangible historical reality characterized by communion with the validly ordained bishop, adherence to the apostolic rule of faith, and celebration of the true Eucharist. Likewise, Saint Irenaeus of Lyons (c. 180 AD), in his monumental treatise *Against Heresies*, argued that truth is found exclusively in the Catholic Church because she alone preserves the unbroken succession of bishops from the Apostles."
        p4 = f"When evaluating opposing arguments, Catholic apologetics demonstrates that alternative models of the Church fail both biblical and historical scrutiny. The Protestant Reformation of the sixteenth century introduced the novel concept of an 'invisible church' composed of all true believers across fragmented denominational lines. However, this concept is entirely absent from the New Testament and the writings of the Church Fathers. A purely invisible church cannot resolve doctrinal disputes (Matthew 18:17), cannot serve as the 'pillar and bulwark of the truth' (1 Timothy 3:15), and cannot manifest the visible unity for which Christ prayed on the night before He died: 'That they may all be one... so that the world may believe that thou hast sent me' (John 17:21). The visible unity of the Catholic Church—governed by the successors of the Apostles under the Petrine primacy of the Bishop of Rome—stands as an enduring miracle and an inescapable witness to the divine origin of Christianity."
        p5 = f"In conclusion, the Catholic doctrine of {title.lower()} offers the only coherent synthesis of biblical prophecy, apostolic practice, patristic testimony, and historical continuity. By remaining in full communion with the Catholic Church, the faithful are not merely joining an earthly organization; they are entering into living communion with the Triune God, receiving the authentic, uncorrupted Gospel of Jesus Christ, and partaking in the very sacraments that communicate sanctifying grace unto eternal life. As Saint Augustine famously declared in the fourth century: 'I would not believe in the Gospel if the authority of the Catholic Church did not move me to do so' (*Against the Epistolam Manichaei*). The Church remains today what she has always been for two thousand years: the ark of salvation, the city set on a mountain, and the Bride of Christ."
        body_text += f"{p1}\n\n{p2}\n\n{p3}\n\n{p4}\n\n{p5}\n"

    return intro_p + body_text


def seed_courses():
    try:
        print("Starting Reusable Arise Course Platform Seeding...", flush=True)

        course = Course.query.filter_by(slug="understanding-the-catholic-church").first()
        if not course:
            course = Course(
                title="Understanding the Catholic Church",
                slug="understanding-the-catholic-church",
                description="A comprehensive, 10-lesson masterclass exploring the divine origin, biblical foundation, historical development, four marks, apostolic authority, sacramental life, and apologetic defense of the Catholic Church.",
                short_description="Master the biblical, historical, and theological foundation of the Catholic Church across 10 in-depth scholarly lessons.",
                thumbnail="images/logo.jpg",
                instructor="Roshen D'silva",
                category="Catholic Ecclesiology & Formation",
                difficulty="Intermediate to Advanced",
                estimated_duration="12 Hours",
                published=True
            )
            db.session.add(course)
            db.session.commit()
            print(f"Created Course: '{course.title}' (ID: {course.id})", flush=True)

        for l_cfg in course_1_lessons:
            lesson = Lesson.query.filter_by(course_id=course.id, slug=l_cfg["slug"]).first()
            content_text = generate_lesson_content(l_cfg["title"])
            if not lesson:
                lesson = Lesson(
                    course_id=course.id,
                    title=l_cfg["title"],
                    slug=l_cfg["slug"],
                    lesson_number=l_cfg["lesson_number"],
                    introduction=f"An in-depth investigation into {l_cfg['title']}.",
                    content=content_text,
                    learning_objectives=l_cfg["objectives"],
                    key_takeaways=l_cfg["takeaways"],
                    scripture_references=l_cfg["scripture"],
                    sources=l_cfg["sources"],
                    estimated_reading_time=l_cfg["reading_time"],
                    published=True
                )
                db.session.add(lesson)
                db.session.commit()
                print(f"  + Added Lesson #{lesson.lesson_number}: '{lesson.title}'", flush=True)
            else:
                # Update existing lesson with unique content, objectives, takeaways, scriptures & sources
                lesson.content = content_text
                lesson.learning_objectives = l_cfg["objectives"]
                lesson.key_takeaways = l_cfg["takeaways"]
                lesson.scripture_references = l_cfg["scripture"]
                lesson.sources = l_cfg["sources"]
                db.session.commit()

            # Seed Quiz & QuizQuestions
            quiz = Quiz.query.filter_by(lesson_id=lesson.id).first()
            if not quiz:
                quiz = Quiz(
                    lesson_id=lesson.id,
                    title=f"Quiz: {lesson.title}",
                    passing_score=70
                )
                db.session.add(quiz)
                db.session.commit()
            else:
                # Clear old questions to replace with unique questions
                QuizQuestion.query.filter_by(quiz_id=quiz.id).delete()
                db.session.commit()

            # Add 5 unique quiz questions per lesson
            for q_item in l_cfg["questions"]:
                q_obj = QuizQuestion(
                    quiz_id=quiz.id,
                    question_text=q_item["question"],
                    option_a=q_item["options"]["A"],
                    option_b=q_item["options"]["B"],
                    option_c=q_item["options"]["C"],
                    option_d=q_item["options"]["D"],
                    correct_option=q_item["correct"],
                    explanation=q_item.get("explanation", "")
                )
                db.session.add(q_obj)
            db.session.commit()

        print("SUCCESS: Finished seeding all 10 unique courses and 50 unique quiz questions!", flush=True)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Course Seeding Exception Notice: {e}", flush=True)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    from app import app
    with app.app_context():
        seed_courses()
